1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Music playing.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:48,620
Well, good morning, Canvas Community Church.

3
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:54,680
Working with a new mic set up this morning, so it might take a little bit for me to not sound like I'm underwater.

4
00:00:57,640 --> 00:01:01,340
It's going to take even longer for me to look like I don't feel like I'm underwater.

5
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:14,520
I know it's been a heavy week for a lot of us, with many tragedies and the anniversary of one of the greatest tragedies of our nation kind of sitting with us.

6
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:24,360
And I, as a pastor, don't know that I can remember a time where I've ever felt as much angst and anticipation about what is going to come out of my mouth next.

7
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,620
And maybe I'm putting that on myself.

8
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:29,780
Maybe that's not from you.

9
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:30,700
Maybe that's from me.

10
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:32,080
Maybe that's not from the Holy Spirit.

11
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:33,140
Maybe that's from Satan.

12
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:33,920
I don't know.

13
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:40,640
But what I do know is I've been on social media and I have seen people say,

14
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:45,840
if your pastor doesn't say blank, you should leave the church.

15
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:52,400
And I've seen people say, if your pastor says blank, you should leave the church.

16
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:09,280
And I just don't think it's a coincidence that we find ourselves in a series about community and a specific message about community around a table centered on the broken body and shed blood of Jesus.

17
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:16,820
Because the reality is we live in a world where there are so many voices in our minds and in our hearts.

18
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:26,380
And we live in a consumer driven world where you are told if your pastor doesn't say the thing that somebody else on the internet that you've never met thinks they should say,

19
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:28,200
then you can just leave and go to another church.

20
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:39,880
And I am just here to pastorally remind you that the thing that Jesus created by breaking his body and shedding his blood was a community around a table.

21
00:02:44,380 --> 00:02:51,940
And if you're waiting for me to say something in particular or waiting for me to not say something in particular so that you can have a reason to leave,

22
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:58,760
I just invite you to come to the table to have conversation because I'm not going to get it right every time.

23
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:01,960
And you're not going to get it right every time.

24
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:09,000
And if we create a culture where the moment we mess up, we get to leave one another's presence and form smaller communities,

25
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:14,920
that's the moment when we lose out on the family of God, mothers and fathers and brothers and sisters.

26
00:03:14,920 --> 00:03:18,900
And so may we just work hard to cultivate a community here.

27
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,080
And it is hard work.

28
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:22,900
It will not be easy.

29
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,420
We will step on each other's toes.

30
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:29,200
We will kick each other in the shins.

31
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:37,960
But at the end of the day, we're family.

32
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:44,360
Around the table with Christ's broken body and shed blood at the center.

33
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:54,240
And if we gather for anything else other than that, may God himself close our doors.

34
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,680
So two things pastorally I want to share real quick before we hop into the message.

35
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:04,500
Just that I have been sitting with, and it's going to tie into the message.

36
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:07,680
It's really, it's crazy how God works in these ways.

37
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:14,360
But just two pastoral implications I want us to think through that I've seen happen on my social media feed,

38
00:04:14,360 --> 00:04:17,760
in my text messages, all of the things that are happening.

39
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:23,280
And the first thing is this, that many of us live way too much of our lives on the Internet.

40
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:32,700
That we prioritize relationships with large groups of digital acquaintances

41
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:39,380
while forsaking real embodied relationships right in our community.

42
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:43,580
And so I think this is part of the problem.

43
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:48,720
We need to put our phones down and we need to be present with the people that God has placed in our lives.

44
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:50,200
Community.

45
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,800
Intentional community.

46
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:56,080
We need to spend our time in the right places and with the right people.

47
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:01,500
And I just, I think we're going to find out later that we weren't created to have

48
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:07,440
so many relationships with so many people that we've never seen and looked in the eye.

49
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:09,460
Like Marissa said, the eye is the lamp into the body.

50
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:11,100
If we can't look into somebody's eyes,

51
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:15,180
then we shouldn't be building our lives on a relationship with them.

52
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:24,880
Secondly, I think many of us have allowed a small minority of people with significant political power

53
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:28,980
to disciple us in the dehumanization of people we disagree with.

54
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:32,800
And that's all across the board.

55
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:41,740
People with significant power and interest in keeping that power have caused us to hate our brothers and sisters

56
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:47,080
who are in the same community as us, where we can't engage in conversation with them or look them in the eye.

57
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:53,200
And what is pressing forward in that is those people keeping power and us hating each other.

58
00:05:54,280 --> 00:06:00,120
And we need to recapture the biblical vision that every human being is created in the image of God

59
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:04,500
and has tremendous value, dignity, and worth solely because of that fact.

60
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:09,000
We are image bearers of God.

61
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:13,500
And we need to see each other that way.

62
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:17,180
So we're over-investing in the wrong types of relationships

63
00:06:17,180 --> 00:06:19,440
and we're putting our hope in the wrong place.

64
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:25,620
And hopefully today's message will give some biblical correction for both of those distorted realities

65
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:31,380
that I think all of us struggle with, myself included, to some degree or another.

66
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:36,680
Today, we are going to hop into week one of the four-week community series.

67
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:46,480
And I think it's helpful that we prioritize these relationships,

68
00:06:46,480 --> 00:06:50,840
these face-to-face relationships around a table because we can lovingly correct each other.

69
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:56,060
And what we are striving for here at Canvas is multi-ethnic, multi-generational community around a table,

70
00:06:56,060 --> 00:07:00,660
which means we will not all look at the same things in the world around us

71
00:07:00,660 --> 00:07:02,480
and see them exactly the same way.

72
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:06,060
And we need to learn to have conversations with one another around a table

73
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:10,740
with Christ's broken body and shed blood at the center to remind us of what we're all about.

74
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:16,640
The best way to engage with these things is in community,

75
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:20,660
is not staring at me and forming opinions about what I'm saying,

76
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:22,960
but going together around a table with others.

77
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:26,440
And so we're intentionally launching small groups right now to do that.

78
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:31,660
There's some tables in the lobby with some new small groups that are going to be out there.

79
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:33,980
If you don't have a small group, you want to join a small group,

80
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:38,540
there's a couple of brand new ones, a Wednesday night one that's open to male and female,

81
00:07:38,540 --> 00:07:41,400
a Wednesday night one that's ladies only, a Friday night one.

82
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:43,580
We've got young adult, we've got all kinds of small groups.

83
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:45,320
So find a place where you can fit in.

84
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:47,000
And it doesn't have to be a church small group.

85
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,700
Just grab a couple people from church and go out for coffee.

86
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:51,260
Do something together.

87
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:54,540
Be embodied in relationship with one another.

88
00:07:54,540 --> 00:07:56,700
Okay, let me pray.

89
00:07:56,700 --> 00:07:59,660
And then we'll do the thing.

90
00:08:02,220 --> 00:08:03,120
Abba in heaven.

91
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:08,060
You are our good father.

92
00:08:08,060 --> 00:08:13,780
And you have brought us together around a table.

93
00:08:13,780 --> 00:08:15,060
You've made space for us.

94
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:16,200
You've made room for us.

95
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:23,420
To come with all of our fear, anger, and sadness and pain.

96
00:08:23,420 --> 00:08:27,960
All of our doubt and disillusion.

97
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:30,260
All of our stuff.

98
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,240
Messy people.

99
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:38,340
God, and you've invited us to come to your table.

100
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:41,120
Not because we deserve it.

101
00:08:41,120 --> 00:08:42,680
Not because we could ever earn it.

102
00:08:42,680 --> 00:08:46,240
But because you have set a place for us at the table.

103
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:48,660
May we not lose sight of that invitation.

104
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:55,820
And God, may you do something supernatural in our midst today.

105
00:08:55,820 --> 00:09:02,260
May you remind us that you have overcome the grave.

106
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:09,220
That there is no fear that we can give in to.

107
00:09:09,220 --> 00:09:13,300
The worst thing that we could fear is death.

108
00:09:13,300 --> 00:09:15,900
And you have conquered death in the grave.

109
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,500
There's nothing to fear.

110
00:09:17,500 --> 00:09:20,200
God, remind us of that this morning.

111
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:20,860
For your glory.

112
00:09:20,860 --> 00:09:21,660
For our good.

113
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:22,780
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

114
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:23,660
Amen.

115
00:09:23,660 --> 00:09:23,880
Amen.

116
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:28,600
Well, many of you know that I was a youth pastor for a while.

117
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:34,380
And what that means in the American church is you have to learn how to play guitar.

118
00:09:34,380 --> 00:09:38,160
You can't be a youth pastor and not know how to play the guitar.

119
00:09:38,160 --> 00:09:39,600
It's just a problem.

120
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:44,800
And so I'm a guitar player, a very novice guitar player.

121
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:45,940
I enjoy playing guitar.

122
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:48,000
I played guitar a lot growing up in church.

123
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:53,120
I really enjoy everything except for keeping the guitar in tune.

124
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:55,960
It's a problem for me.

125
00:09:56,080 --> 00:10:01,500
See, guitars are intentionally crafted for specific strings to be in tune to specific notes.

126
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:05,860
And when that happens, they play all together in harmony.

127
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:06,380
That happened.

128
00:10:06,380 --> 00:10:07,640
You guys hear that?

129
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:10,340
I'm going to try not to forget.

130
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:11,680
It's not in tune.

131
00:10:11,680 --> 00:10:13,040
Okay.

132
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:19,060
So when all the strings are tuned together, they play in harmony.

133
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:26,880
But no matter how good the guitar or how meticulous the guitar player, external factors are always going to pull those strings out of tune.

134
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:35,220
No matter how hard you try, no matter how hard you work, no matter how on top of it you are, eventually a string or two will find its way out of tune.

135
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:39,320
And then people like me are in deep, deep trouble.

136
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:42,340
Because I am what you would call pitch poor.

137
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:45,780
It's not tone deaf.

138
00:10:45,780 --> 00:10:48,460
Pitch poor.

139
00:10:48,460 --> 00:10:49,940
There's tone deaf and there's pitch poor.

140
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:52,440
And tone deaf people can't even tell the guitar is out of tune.

141
00:10:52,440 --> 00:10:56,440
They just keep playing and singing and smiling because they don't know it's out of tune.

142
00:10:56,440 --> 00:11:01,920
Pitch poor people can tell that it's out of tune but can't do anything to get it back into tune.

143
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,360
That's me.

144
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:04,760
I'm like, oh, my guitar is out of tune.

145
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:06,840
And I'm twisting and that's making it more out of tune.

146
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:07,900
And I'm like, well, that's not right.

147
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:09,040
But I don't know how to make it right.

148
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:09,760
I'm in trouble here.

149
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:15,840
And so I would often have to have somebody with good pitch help me get the guitar back in tune.

150
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:18,360
And that was helpful and good.

151
00:11:18,360 --> 00:11:25,900
But I still always wanted to have the digital tuner to make sure it was, like, perfect.

152
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:27,600
Again, because I'm pitch poor.

153
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:28,980
And I trust the person with good pitch.

154
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:30,300
And I'm like, yeah, that sounds way better.

155
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:31,660
But, like, how right are they?

156
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:34,660
And so I would check it with the digital tuner.

157
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:40,340
And it was both the person with the good tune and the digital tuner that would

158
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:43,860
help me make sure that I could play my guitar in tune.

159
00:11:43,860 --> 00:11:48,980
And I think in the same way as human beings, we were created for intimate community with

160
00:11:48,980 --> 00:11:49,480
one another.

161
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:56,300
We were intended for our uniqueness to harmonize with one another according to God's good design.

162
00:11:57,340 --> 00:12:07,040
But just like guitars, there are external factors that cause most of us to drift away from community and

163
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:10,220
and playing bad notes to the world around us.

164
00:12:10,220 --> 00:12:15,180
And just like I needed somebody to come help me get my guitar back in tune.

165
00:12:15,680 --> 00:12:22,460
We need one another in community to make sure that we are being the person that God has made us to be.

166
00:12:23,380 --> 00:12:30,040
And even in that vision, as we come together and remind ourselves of who we're created to be, we're all prone to wander.

167
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,180
We're all prone to drift away.

168
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:42,800
And so we need what I would call communion, Christ's broken body and shed blood as that check, that digital tuner that's going to make sure you're like, yeah, we come together in community.

169
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:44,280
We remind ourselves of what's true.

170
00:12:44,440 --> 00:12:47,220
We encourage one another to play the note that God's called us to play.

171
00:12:47,220 --> 00:12:54,620
And then we put Christ Jesus at the center of the table to make sure that we do not drift away from who God's made us to be.

172
00:12:54,620 --> 00:13:00,720
And so this morning, I want to spend most of my time talking about those two things, community and communion.

173
00:13:00,720 --> 00:13:09,340
Community around the table, small group of people who are living life together, who are reminding each other of who God has called us to be.

174
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:15,440
And then just in case, because we're prone to wander, we get off track.

175
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:19,280
We keep Jesus at the center of the table.

176
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:23,100
We keep reminding ourselves of what this whole thing is about.

177
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:29,200
Because when we get together in community, we can easily drift away from God's vision for our life.

178
00:13:29,200 --> 00:13:33,880
That's kind of the thesis for the rest of our time together.

179
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:34,320
Sound good?

180
00:13:34,320 --> 00:13:37,080
We're going to be bouncing all over different Scriptures.

181
00:13:37,260 --> 00:13:40,620
We're not going to have like one passage that we land in today.

182
00:13:40,620 --> 00:13:43,920
And so let's just stick around for the ride.

183
00:13:43,920 --> 00:13:44,640
All right?

184
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:47,300
First thing I want to look at is the creator's design.

185
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:53,940
Just like a guitar is created to play in tune with one another, human beings are created to live in community with one another.

186
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:55,760
Genesis chapter 1, verse 26.

187
00:13:57,220 --> 00:14:19,380
Says, then God said, let us, notice the yellow words here, let us make mankind in our image, in our likeness, so that they together may rule over the fish in the sea and the birds in the sky, over the livestock and over the wild animals and over all creatures that move along the ground.

188
00:14:19,380 --> 00:14:28,140
So God created mankind in his own image, in the image of God, he created, say it with me, them.

189
00:14:28,140 --> 00:14:32,100
Male and female, he created them.

190
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:41,580
God blessed, say it with me, them, and said to them, be fruitful and increase in number, fill the earth and subdue it.

191
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:48,920
From the very first pages of Scripture, we see the doctrine that the church has called the Trinity, that God is one in three persons.

192
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:53,580
He says, let us create man in our image.

193
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:55,400
It's communal language.

194
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:59,500
God lovingly chooses to create us in his image.

195
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:07,260
And since his image is relational, us, let us make mankind in our image.

196
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:12,740
Since God's image is relational, that means that we are created as relational beings.

197
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:16,980
By nature, we are intended to live in community with one another.

198
00:15:16,980 --> 00:15:21,960
Living in community is a necessary part of bearing the image of God to the world around us.

199
00:15:21,960 --> 00:15:24,140
I love how Gary Brashears puts it.

200
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:28,140
He says, God is a family who makes family.

201
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:35,240
So what we see on the very first page of Scripture, let us make mankind in our image.

202
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:38,720
Put them together in community to rule the earth and subdue it.

203
00:15:38,720 --> 00:15:41,320
God is a family who makes family.

204
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:47,000
Later on the first page of Scripture, we read God say, it is not good.

205
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:48,900
This is the first time we hear those words.

206
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:49,740
Everything is good.

207
00:15:49,740 --> 00:15:50,500
Everything is good.

208
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:51,220
Everything is good.

209
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:52,780
And then we hear God say, it is not good.

210
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:53,600
What is not good?

211
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:56,780
It is not good for man to be alone.

212
00:15:58,260 --> 00:16:08,020
So we see in God's design that we were made to live in community with one another, face-to-face, embodied relationships with one another.

213
00:16:08,020 --> 00:16:09,180
It's part of who God is.

214
00:16:09,180 --> 00:16:10,240
It's his very character.

215
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:10,960
It's his very nature.

216
00:16:10,960 --> 00:16:15,620
And in order for us to image God, we have to live together in community.

217
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:20,960
And then the first thing he says is not good is that man is alone.

218
00:16:20,960 --> 00:16:26,600
Healthy biblical community requires male and female relationship in order to image God.

219
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:28,420
We cannot image God alone.

220
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:34,340
And yes, part of this design is reproductive, which is a different sermon for a different day.

221
00:16:34,340 --> 00:16:40,800
Part of it is fill the earth and subdue it.

222
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:47,700
But in other parts of Scripture, it's made clear that there are a number of reasons why singleness might be God's calling for individual,

223
00:16:47,700 --> 00:16:56,920
which means as a church, we should be pursuing male and female relationships and communities that aren't just built on husband and wife.

224
00:16:56,920 --> 00:17:04,420
Creating communities that are welcome spaces for married couples, singles, widows, and other unique situations.

225
00:17:05,220 --> 00:17:12,720
Much of the American church culture has put the modern vision of the nuclear family as God's ideal, the center of God's design.

226
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:19,920
And that has left many people who don't neatly fit into that category feeling like they're on the outside looking in.

227
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:31,180
And what we've been invited to as brothers and sisters and mothers and fathers and aunts and uncles is a community around the table that includes people from all different situations and circumstances.

228
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:40,480
So from the first page of Scripture, we can see that to be fully human or to be in tune with the creator's design, we are to live in community with one another.

229
00:17:40,480 --> 00:17:46,000
But from the second page of Scripture, doesn't take very long, right?

230
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:53,600
From the second page of Scripture, this in-tune instrument that God created begins to get pulled out of tune.

231
00:17:53,600 --> 00:18:01,760
These are the obstacles that we face when it comes to creating community around the table.

232
00:18:01,820 --> 00:18:07,580
And after this week, we all know there's no obstacles at all to creating unified community around the table.

233
00:18:07,580 --> 00:18:11,480
Genesis 3.

234
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:21,620
When the woman saw the fruit of the tree was good for food and pleasing to the eyes and desirable for gaining wisdom, she took some and she ate it.

235
00:18:21,980 --> 00:18:24,040
She also gave some to her husband.

236
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:28,360
Listen to this gentleman who was with her and he ate it.

237
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:32,000
Then the eyes of both of them were open and listen to this.

238
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,120
They realized they were naked.

239
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:39,700
So they sewed fig leaves together and made the coverings for themselves.

240
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:42,820
Then the man and his wife heard the sound of the Lord.

241
00:18:42,820 --> 00:18:45,680
So the first thing they do is they hide parts of themselves from one another.

242
00:18:45,680 --> 00:18:48,840
The second thing they do is they hide themselves from God.

243
00:18:48,840 --> 00:18:55,440
The man and his wife heard the sound of the Lord walking in the garden in the cool of day and they hid from the Lord among the trees in the garden.

244
00:18:56,180 --> 00:19:06,000
The man said, the woman you put here with me, she gave me some fruit for the tree and I ate it.

245
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:16,860
So can we see how from the very beginning pages of Scripture we begin to get out of tune with our design to live in vulnerable, transparent community with one another, self-giving, sacrificial love with one another?

246
00:19:16,860 --> 00:19:22,360
Immediately between page one and page two, this is in tune instrument, page two, out of tune instrument.

247
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:24,940
That woman you gave me, I didn't ask for her.

248
00:19:24,940 --> 00:19:26,760
I didn't need this, right?

249
00:19:26,760 --> 00:19:28,000
I'm good on my own.

250
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:41,040
And here's the thing, when Adam sees Eve for the first time, naked right in front of him, does he say, I didn't ask for this?

251
00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:51,360
No, he says, woman, bone of my bone, flesh of my flesh, thank you, Lord, for this good gift.

252
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:57,380
And then one page later, he says, I didn't ask for her, right?

253
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:11,420
And so there's these obstacles that immediately come into our lives that keep us from living into the vision that God has created us for, community around a table, tight-knit, close family, relationships with one another.

254
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:13,160
We were created for this thing.

255
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:18,040
But immediately shame comes in and we move away from one another.

256
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:25,200
We begin to hide parts of ourselves from one another and blame one another for the predicament that we find ourselves in.

257
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:28,720
Sound familiar?

258
00:20:28,720 --> 00:20:41,740
This is on repeat throughout Scripture and now even in our day and age, we begin to hide parts of ourselves from one another and we blame each other for the predicament that we are in.

259
00:20:41,740 --> 00:20:47,240
And we put on a false front, a self on social media that this is who I am.

260
00:20:47,240 --> 00:20:49,700
And behind closed doors, we know that that's not fully true.

261
00:20:49,700 --> 00:20:54,660
And we're not comfortable and safe enough to be who God made us to be in community with other people.

262
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:57,140
And so we create a false image for ourselves.

263
00:20:57,140 --> 00:21:05,060
We cover ourselves up and we point in fingers and we blame shift about what the problem really is in creation.

264
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:10,640
And here's the heartbreaking part is the problem is sin.

265
00:21:13,320 --> 00:21:13,980
The problem is sin.

266
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:20,440
Sin on the right, sin on the left, sin down the middle, sin in a different country.

267
00:21:20,440 --> 00:21:21,580
It's sin.

268
00:21:21,580 --> 00:21:22,720
The problem is sin.

269
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:29,560
And we create an image of ourselves that makes us more righteous than the other group of people that we have demonized.

270
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:33,660
And we make the problem no longer sin, but their sin.

271
00:21:33,660 --> 00:21:36,240
This woman that you gave me.

272
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:38,640
It's their sin that's the problem.

273
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:39,660
It's not my problem.

274
00:21:39,660 --> 00:21:40,580
It's their problem.

275
00:21:40,580 --> 00:21:43,680
And the problem with that is it's all of our problem.

276
00:21:43,680 --> 00:21:50,740
And we need God to intervene because apart from him, we cannot solve the problem.

277
00:21:50,740 --> 00:21:51,300
Oh, boy.

278
00:21:51,300 --> 00:21:51,560
Amen.

279
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:02,920
All of humanity has had significant obstacles to genuine community since the fall.

280
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:04,680
We can see it from the very beginning of Scripture.

281
00:22:04,680 --> 00:22:10,420
But I think our current cultural moment creates some obstacles that are unique to us.

282
00:22:10,420 --> 00:22:20,480
Some cultural concepts that weren't true in biblical times, but that are true now that make community even harder than it was in biblical times.

283
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:27,920
Like I said earlier, our culture has idealized the concept of the nuclear family, which is a relatively modern development in human history.

284
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:37,960
We've decided that the ideal biblical vision for the family is a mom and a dad and two and a half kids in a suburban house with a white picket fence.

285
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:39,080
Right?

286
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:42,240
Isn't this the vision that we have been taught?

287
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,780
That this is what God wants for you?

288
00:22:43,780 --> 00:22:45,060
This is what God wants for everyone?

289
00:22:45,180 --> 00:22:52,780
And the image of the ideal family structure has caused us to become even more isolated and internally focused.

290
00:22:52,780 --> 00:23:03,780
Because when it's just four of us in this house behind a fence, then my life pursuit becomes providing for these four human beings and everybody else becomes an enemy and an obstacle to my joy.

291
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:08,120
And we were created for community with one another.

292
00:23:08,120 --> 00:23:10,860
I'm not saying that the nuclear family is a bad thing.

293
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:17,360
I have a wife and a child and a dog and an invisible fence because I'm godly.

294
00:23:17,360 --> 00:23:19,040
I don't want you to see that I don't want you.

295
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:26,360
It's not a bad thing.

296
00:23:26,360 --> 00:23:31,960
I'm just saying it creates cultural obstacles because we isolate and we move further and further away from one another.

297
00:23:31,960 --> 00:23:33,260
Amen.

298
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:43,680
When the biblical vision is that we would invite one another into community, but our culture has caused us to push each other further and further away.

299
00:23:43,680 --> 00:23:53,740
We isolate and we hide behind our locked doors and we hide behind our glowing square rectangles.

300
00:23:54,700 --> 00:24:05,960
And C.S. Lewis in his book, The Great Divorce, when he's artistically describing what hell is like, it's people moving further and further and further away from one another.

301
00:24:05,960 --> 00:24:12,640
Because every time they're close enough to see somebody, that person rubs them the wrong way and they keep moving further and further away.

302
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:17,820
And C.S. Lewis says that's the vision of hell because we were created for community with one another.

303
00:24:17,820 --> 00:24:20,160
And we culturally are doing this.

304
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:22,460
We're moving further and further away from one another.

305
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:24,080
We're isolating ourselves.

306
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:31,520
And we're investing in fake relationships with digital people that have no faces in front of them.

307
00:24:31,520 --> 00:24:34,980
This is killing community.

308
00:24:34,980 --> 00:24:39,400
It's killing God's vision that we should live together in harmony.

309
00:24:39,400 --> 00:24:51,740
The image of the ideal family structures caused us to become even more isolated and internally focused than other times in history.

310
00:24:52,060 --> 00:24:56,640
Our primary concern becomes making sure that our individual family has enough.

311
00:24:56,640 --> 00:25:00,440
I'm going to step on a toe real quick and then move on, right?

312
00:25:00,440 --> 00:25:09,520
On top of that, we invest incredible amounts of money and time, often going into debt and burning ourselves out to give our children whatever they ask for.

313
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:13,720
Even if the thing they ask for doesn't lead them to abundant life in Jesus.

314
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:24,500
So this is cultural, biblical problems that keep us from living in community.

315
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:30,380
And in contrast, the image we see in the Bible is culturally different.

316
00:25:30,380 --> 00:25:35,140
And what I don't want to tell you, and I'm feeling, I'll get there.

317
00:25:36,300 --> 00:25:40,480
I'm just, I'm always, I don't like creating tension, but sometimes you need to create tension.

318
00:25:40,480 --> 00:25:44,160
So I'm going to put my, yeah, okay.

319
00:25:45,520 --> 00:25:53,940
So in contrast to this image, when the Bible refers to family or household like we read in Ephesians this morning, it's not referring to the nuclear family.

320
00:25:53,940 --> 00:25:56,940
It's not referring to one mom, one dad, and kids.

321
00:25:57,900 --> 00:26:04,600
It's referring to a broader network of people that have decided to live in community together for the good of one another.

322
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:07,600
These are blood relatives and not blood relatives.

323
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:16,340
They have made an intentional decision to live in community with one another, to form a larger family unit, to pursue the common good for one another.

324
00:26:16,340 --> 00:26:17,480
Now, don't freak out.

325
00:26:17,480 --> 00:26:19,640
Were you freaking out?

326
00:26:19,640 --> 00:26:21,280
Oh, okay.

327
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:21,760
That's good.

328
00:26:22,440 --> 00:26:30,600
Don't freak out, because what I'm not saying is that you have to ditch the American cultural system and pattern your lives after first century Jewish family structure.

329
00:26:30,600 --> 00:26:35,500
That would be a quick way to kill our church.

330
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:37,520
It's like, quit being 21st century Americans.

331
00:26:37,520 --> 00:26:40,680
Let's all go back to first century Judaism, and it'll be great, right?

332
00:26:40,680 --> 00:26:43,440
That's not the solution to our problem.

333
00:26:47,740 --> 00:26:55,040
But I want to bring this up, because I think it's helpful for us to understand that when we read about family and about household and about community in the Scriptures,

334
00:26:55,040 --> 00:26:59,540
what the Bible means by community and family is something very different than what we mean.

335
00:26:59,540 --> 00:27:09,560
And because of this cultural framework, the early church would be held in the context of a household centered around the communion table.

336
00:27:09,560 --> 00:27:11,960
Like, this is just the air that they're breathing.

337
00:27:11,960 --> 00:27:17,480
When they go to church, they are going to church in their neighborhood, in somebody's house, right?

338
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:20,120
Like, this is the cultural framework that they have.

339
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:31,400
And now what we do is we drive 20 to 30 minutes away from our house to go to a church because we like the vibe, the cafe, the children's ministry, or the set list of the worship team.

340
00:27:31,400 --> 00:27:36,880
We sit quietly in rows and center our time around one person giving a TED Talk for Jesus.

341
00:27:40,340 --> 00:27:42,720
Here I am, and here you are.

342
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:48,720
And the moment that the person giving the TED Talk says something we disagree with,

343
00:27:48,720 --> 00:27:53,580
or the moment when someone in leadership posts something on social media that we don't like,

344
00:27:53,580 --> 00:27:56,380
we fire up the Google machine and we go looking for a different church.

345
00:27:56,380 --> 00:28:05,980
This system is not created to help us cultivate genuine, authentic community with one another.

346
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:09,320
If anything, it works against it.

347
00:28:09,320 --> 00:28:16,260
It keeps us busy and isolated, connected to a church organization, but not connected to the people who are the church.

348
00:28:16,260 --> 00:28:19,140
Love how John Mark Homer puts it.

349
00:28:19,140 --> 00:28:24,960
He says, for the first time in human history, you can go to church without ever experiencing community.

350
00:28:26,400 --> 00:28:34,540
And so I just, I point all of this out to say, I think there are some external factors that are causing some of our guitar strings to go out of tune.

351
00:28:34,540 --> 00:28:45,420
We're being pushed out of community and pushed into a fake version of community, and it's causing a lot of chaos in our lives.

352
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:57,120
And I just want us to notice it and lean into getting back in tune, sitting around a table with other people who are embodied human beings that live in our community,

353
00:28:57,120 --> 00:29:02,140
that may or may not agree with everything that we think or believe about the Bible or politics,

354
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:09,260
but that we are seeking Jesus together, face-to-face, in real, genuine relationships.

355
00:29:11,260 --> 00:29:19,600
So because these are the obstacles we're facing, we need to supplement Sunday morning church attendance with intentional pursuit of authentic community in smaller settings.

356
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:22,500
I'm not saying that this thing that we do here is bad.

357
00:29:22,500 --> 00:29:23,600
It's not bad.

358
00:29:24,700 --> 00:29:33,160
In many ways, it's a cultural, missiological expression of seeking to reach the church outside the walls of the gospel.

359
00:29:33,160 --> 00:29:35,940
Because people are used to coming in and sitting in theater seating.

360
00:29:35,940 --> 00:29:37,960
So we just said, hey, here's our theater seats.

361
00:29:37,960 --> 00:29:39,820
Come sit down, we'll talk to you about Jesus, right?

362
00:29:39,820 --> 00:29:41,400
It's not all bad.

363
00:29:41,400 --> 00:29:47,280
All I'm saying is it's keeping us from fully experiencing the community that we were created for.

364
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:53,580
And this isn't easy or convenient, and it will take consistent sacrifice,

365
00:29:53,580 --> 00:29:58,680
but I'm here to tell you from 10 plus years of experience of doing this thing that it's worth it.

366
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:08,700
Marissa and I had a small group in our house in Pennsylvania about, we probably met 49 weeks a year.

367
00:30:08,700 --> 00:30:11,440
Literally, we took two or three weeks off.

368
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:16,560
And there were so many weeks that got to Wednesday night, and I looked at Marissa, and I said,

369
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:17,540
can we please cancel?

370
00:30:17,540 --> 00:30:18,880
I don't want these people in my house.

371
00:30:19,640 --> 00:30:22,420
I'm so tired.

372
00:30:22,420 --> 00:30:28,480
I just want to turn on a TV and not be present with any human beings.

373
00:30:28,480 --> 00:30:33,460
But we've made this decision with one another to live in community.

374
00:30:33,460 --> 00:30:35,920
We'd agreed upon it.

375
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:38,680
And we kept pursuing it, even when it was hard.

376
00:30:38,680 --> 00:30:42,880
And sometimes we'd all show up in sweatpants, and we'd all say, none of us want to be here.

377
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:47,900
And we'd all talk about what had happened in our weeks, and we'd pray together.

378
00:30:49,640 --> 00:30:58,300
And now I haven't sat in that living room in five years, and if I pick up the phone and call any single one of those people,

379
00:30:58,300 --> 00:31:03,740
they will answer it, and they will do whatever I need them to do, no matter what it costs them,

380
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:09,540
because we formed family around the table centered on Jesus, and it was not easy.

381
00:31:09,540 --> 00:31:14,080
It took consistent sacrifice.

382
00:31:15,020 --> 00:31:16,420
But, man, is it worth it.

383
00:31:16,420 --> 00:31:23,100
And I know there are some of you in this room who I have sat in your living room consistently and regularly,

384
00:31:23,100 --> 00:31:30,720
and if anything happened to me or my family, you guys would fight my actual family to provide for me and my family

385
00:31:30,720 --> 00:31:32,180
before they got a chance to do it.

386
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:34,540
And it hasn't been easy.

387
00:31:34,540 --> 00:31:41,680
Just had to look one of them in the eye.

388
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:42,480
Shoot.

389
00:31:42,480 --> 00:31:47,840
They're family.

390
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:56,500
And that's what God invites us to do to become family around the table centered on the broken body and shed blood of Jesus.

391
00:31:59,500 --> 00:32:00,440
Okay, sorry.

392
00:32:00,440 --> 00:32:04,480
Now, we've pitched this series.

393
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:10,240
We've pitched this series as a series around the life and teachings of Jesus,

394
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:13,240
and we haven't talked about Jesus once yet from this avenue.

395
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:19,880
And so I want to look at Christ's example for how he modeled his life around community

396
00:32:19,880 --> 00:32:25,040
and the invitation that he gave to his followers to come to the table.

397
00:32:25,780 --> 00:32:31,720
Now, it wouldn't take you too long into a reading through the Gospels to see that Jesus prioritizes community with other people.

398
00:32:31,720 --> 00:32:35,080
Like, we did a series on solitude.

399
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:38,920
There's times and places where God goes, where Jesus goes away from people.

400
00:32:38,920 --> 00:32:41,360
But that's not the norm.

401
00:32:41,360 --> 00:32:46,420
That's a spiritual rhythm that he had to stay connected to the Father,

402
00:32:46,420 --> 00:32:51,060
but regularly he is with people, and regularly people are exhausting him.

403
00:32:54,240 --> 00:32:55,720
Robert Karras puts it this way.

404
00:32:55,720 --> 00:32:57,820
He has a commentary on Luke, and he says,

405
00:32:57,820 --> 00:33:04,240
In Luke's Gospel, Jesus is either going to a meal, at a meal, or coming from a meal.

406
00:33:04,240 --> 00:33:10,940
And now you know why I'm eager to follow this rabbi named Jesus.

407
00:33:18,700 --> 00:33:24,960
Not only does he regularly model this for us, but he redefines what community actually is, what family actually is.

408
00:33:24,960 --> 00:33:27,280
We talked about this last week, so I'm not going to spend a lot of time.

409
00:33:27,280 --> 00:33:31,680
But his mother and sisters and brothers come looking for him, and people are like,

410
00:33:31,680 --> 00:33:33,660
Hey, your nuclear family is out there.

411
00:33:33,660 --> 00:33:35,580
They need to talk to you.

412
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:40,340
And he says, who is my mothers, my brothers, and my sisters?

413
00:33:40,340 --> 00:33:41,060
He says, those.

414
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:42,080
He points to his disciples.

415
00:33:42,080 --> 00:33:44,740
So he's in a room crowded, full of people.

416
00:33:45,860 --> 00:33:47,720
His nuclear family is outside.

417
00:33:47,720 --> 00:33:50,660
There's a crowd inside, and his disciples are around him.

418
00:33:50,660 --> 00:33:52,400
And he points to his disciples.

419
00:33:52,400 --> 00:33:59,740
And he says, those who do the will of my father are my mothers, and my brothers, and my sisters.

420
00:33:59,740 --> 00:34:09,420
So family begins to be created for followers of Jesus around a table with Jesus at the center,

421
00:34:09,580 --> 00:34:14,180
and our brothers and sisters becoming those who are surrendered to his example.

422
00:34:14,180 --> 00:34:15,120
Does that make sense?

423
00:34:15,120 --> 00:34:19,480
From the example of Christ, we can begin to draw out some practical principles.

424
00:34:19,480 --> 00:34:25,320
Christ regularly invested in different sized communities at different levels.

425
00:34:25,320 --> 00:34:31,060
And this is where I want to talk about where we get out of whack as human beings in the 21st century.

426
00:34:31,820 --> 00:34:34,100
Jesus had his closest three disciples.

427
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:35,960
He had 12 disciples.

428
00:34:35,960 --> 00:34:40,200
And he had a larger group of disciples, and then this massive group called the crowd.

429
00:34:40,200 --> 00:34:49,480
And regularly and intentionally, he spent more and more time with the three and the 12 and the 70 to 120.

430
00:34:49,480 --> 00:34:55,360
As things got closer and closer to the crucifixion, he was investing in these relationships.

431
00:34:55,360 --> 00:35:01,440
I saw a meme not too long ago that was like, Jesus is a dude in his 30s, and he has three best friends.

432
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:04,280
And that's why I want to follow him, right?

433
00:35:04,280 --> 00:35:07,600
Like how many of us have three best friends that we can call?

434
00:35:07,600 --> 00:35:09,380
And like it takes investment.

435
00:35:09,380 --> 00:35:10,660
It takes intentionality.

436
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:18,100
And these groups of people fit into a broader kind of social science category called Dunbar's Number.

437
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:22,800
And it's funny that Jesus kind of knows how humanity works.

438
00:35:25,360 --> 00:35:35,480
And so social experts have said that human development, really, you can only have three, one to three very close friends.

439
